{"id": "676acd03-1810-41c1-a689-5d2f129cd0e6", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T10:15:32.771864", "source": "user", "parent_id": "9299e534-d9fe-4316-ab2a-0087717359a2", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "Before we design anything, let's build a shared picture of what we actually have and what's genuinely uncertain. Please do three things and report back:\n\n1) Repository/data inspection — Look at what's available in /workspace and any provided documentation. Confirm whether DrivAerNet++ is actually present or only referenced as a known dataset. Report anything concrete we can extract: the exact 26-parameter list, the drag target (e.g., drag coefficient Cd) and its units, sample counts, whether parameter values span body styles (fastback/notchback/estateback) or are encoded as separate fields, and whether ground-truth drag comes from CFD runs in the dataset.\n\n2) Proposed approach (outline only, not the full design) — Sketch how you'd answer the two-part question: (a) does predictive performance improve with training set size, and (b) does it differ across model architectures. This should cover candidate model families (e.g., linear/ridge, polynomial, random forest, GPR, small MLP), a set of training-set sizes (including the practical ceiling given whatever data we confirm), the performance metric (RMSE and/or R² on Cd, likely log-transform given drag magnitude spread), and how you'd make results comparable across architectures (same folds/splits, same scaling, repeated seeds).\n\n3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties — List the assumptions you'd be forced to make, and flag anything you cannot verify from available materials (e.g., exact DrivAerNet++ version, parameter ranges, whether a held-out CFD test set exists, compute constraints). Distinguish what is a fixed requirement versus your suggested choice.\n\nDo not write the full final design yet — I only need the picture, the outline, and the honest uncertainties so we can lock down an approach.", "required_handoff_stage": "plan", "approach_approved": false, "public_task": {"question": "Does the predictive performance of surrogate models based on parametric data improve with increased training set size and differ across model architectures in aerodynamic drag prediction? This experiment uses aerodynamic car designs characterized by 26 parameters—as provided in the DrivAerNet++ dataset, which includes diverse configurations such as fastback, notchback, and estateback—for assessing performance scalability and model-specific behavior.", "context": "Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.", "environment": {"runtime": "bubblewrap", "network": "disabled", "repository": "/workspace", "python": "python3", "software_environment": "system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled", "datasets": [], "gpu_enabled": false, "cpu_affinity_count": 224, "note": "Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment."}, "researcher_topics": [], "question_budget": 6, "actor_step_budget": 60, "continue_after_initial_plan": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
