1. Learning-curve figure: test RMSE (mean±std) vs n, per model, log-log, with GPR partial range marked.
2. Table of scaling exponents b with bootstrap CIs and significance flags.
3. Table of paired bootstrap ΔRMSE between every model pair at every ladder size, with CIs.
4. Ranking-stability summary across n.
5. Per-body-style RMSE breakdown (fastback/notchback/estateback) at the ceiling size.
6. Robustness appendix: log-Cd sensitivity run (Ridge, MLP) and any data-cleaning/halted-branch reports.
7. Written verdict on Claim A (size-scaling: yes/no, with exponent) and Claim B (architecture differences: yes/no, which models, at which n), with the control table above as evidence mapping.", "evidence": "Sandbox inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; filesystem-wide search for csv/parquet/h5/json/drivaer* found no data files; network disabled (URLError name resolution failure); Python 3.14.4, pip 25.1.1, no sklearn/numpy/scipy/pandas/torch; 224 CPU cores, ~2 TB RAM, no GPU. task.json documents the task as design-only ('track.md: Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'). Researcher directives for this handoff: convert each unverifiable-data uncertainty into a contingency branch; give size ladder as function of N_total with GPR cap ~2000 flagged as partial-curve; honor-or-create split policy; load-time Cd-vs-CdA and parameter-count gates; do not assert unverified DrivAerNet++ facts as truth; keep fixed-vs-suggested distinction; end with the two testable claims and how controls support them.", "assumptions": ["Dataset must be mounted externally; DrivAerNet++ not present in sandbox and network is offline.", "Rows are tabular (26 numeric params + body style + Cd); if only meshes exist, experiment cannot run without CFD (out of scope).", "Body style is a separate categorical column by default, with the baked-in fallback branch.", "CFD-derived Cd is the per-row ground-truth target, enforced by a load-time plausibility gate.", "Row count is a low-thousands anchor; the ladder adapts via n_max = N_total - N_test.", "No canonical split assumed; honored only if the dataset ships one."], "uncertainties": ["Exact 26-parameter names/semantics/ranges/units - read from mounted metadata at run time; parameter-count gate halts if !=26.", "Actual N_total sets the ladder ceiling; ladder defined as a function of N_total.", "Body-style encoding branch (separate column vs baked-in) resolved by schema inspection.", "Whether the dataset ships a canonical test split (honor-or-create policy).", "Target column naming (cd vs CdA) resolved by load-time gate; halt if only CdA exists.", "Execution inside this sandbox is impossible offline (no data, no packages); a provisioned environment and mounted data are required for a future run."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-7ac80105c9264085b822f3c2f7f44558", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-7ac80105c9264085b822f3c2f7f44558", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"stage": "result", "summary": "Deliver complete experiment design with contingencies and claims", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Surrogate-Model Performance vs. Training Size and Architecture for DrivAerNet++ Aerodynamic Drag Prediction\n\n## 0. Scope and status\n\nDesign-only deliverable. **No experiment was run**; no data is present in the sandbox (only `/workspace/task.json`), network is disabled, and no ML packages are installed. All numeric facts about the data are treated as **contingencies with fallbacks**, not verified truths. The design is fully executable once the dataset is mounted and a Python env (numpy/scipy/sklearn; an MLP via sklearn MLPRegressor \u2014 no torch needed) is provisioned.\n\n## 1. Fixed requirements vs. suggested choices\n\n**Fixed requirements (from the task; hold unconditionally):**\n- F1. Input: one design = 26 numeric parameters, as supplied by DrivAerNet++ parametric rows.\n- F2. Dataset spans fastback, notchback, estateback configurations.\n- F3. Target: aerodynamic drag, i.e., the drag coefficient Cd (dimensionless).\n- F4. Question has two parts: (a) does predictive performance improve with training-set size; (b) does it differ across model architectures.\n- F5. Tabular/parametric surrogate models only (no mesh/graph networks).\n\n**Suggested choices (preregistered by this design; adjustable only by explicit decision):**\n- S1. Frozen 80/20 stratified split (honor a dataset-provided split if one exists \u2014 see \u00a74).\n- S2. Training-size ladder as a function of N_total (\u00a75).\n- S3. Model set: Ridge, polynomial-degree-2 Ridge, Random Forest, HistGradientBoosting (optional), Gaussian Process Regression (capped), small MLP (\u00a76).\n- S4. Metric set: RMSE primary (in Cd units), MAE and R\u00b2 secondary, per-body-style test subsets; log-Cd as a stated robustness check (\u00a77).\n- S5. Repeated-seed scheme: 5 replicate training-subset draws per (model, size); 5 MLP weight-init seeds nested; report mean \u00b1 std (\u00a78).\n- S6. Analysis: log-log scaling exponent with bootstrap CI; paired bootstrap CI on RMSE differences; ranking stability across n (\u00a79).\n\n## 2. Testable claims (the two claims the design is built to support)\n\n- **Claim A (size-scaling):** For each architecture, test-set RMSE on Cd decreases with increasing training-set size; the per-model scaling exponent b in RMSE(n) \u2248 a\u00b7n^b is significantly negative, and its magnitude (and stability) is reported. Supported by: the frozen test set reused at every n (\u00a74), the log-spaced ladder spanning a 2-orders-of-magnitude range (\u00a75), repeated subsample draws so the curve is a distribution not a single draw (\u00a78), and bootstrap CIs on b (\u00a79).\n- **Claim B (architecture difference):** At matched training sizes, model architectures achieve different test RMSE, and the ranking/differences are assessed with paired bootstrap CIs so the comparison is statistically grounded, not just point estimates. Supported by: identical test set, identical preprocessing, identical seeds and subsamples across models (\u00a78 pairing), and matched n so \"better because it saw more data\" is excluded (\u00a75).\n\n## 3. Data schema handling with explicit fallbacks\n\nAt load time, the loader reads the mounted dataset table and applies the first branch that matches:\n\n- **C-Schema-1 (expected):** a single tabular file (CSV/parquet/HDF5) with one row per design containing \u226526 numeric parameter columns, a body-style column, and a Cd column. Use as-is.\n- **C-Schema-2 (params file + results file):** design IDs in a params table and CFD results in a separate table; join on the design ID key.\n- **C-Schema-3 (target naming):** target column named `cd`. If a `CdA`/drag-area column exists but no `cd`, prefer `cd`; if only `CdA` exists, **halt and flag**: the task target is Cd, so we must not silently substitute CdA; the design would require the Cd column (a stated assumption A3).\n- **C-Schema-4 (feature count):** if the parameter vector is not exactly 26, **halt and flag** rather than silently proceeding (F1 is fixed; a 40-parameter variant would need a scope decision).\n- **C-Schema-5 (rows):** drop rows with any NaN in the 26 params or in Cd; drop exact duplicate parameter vectors differing in Cd (data-integrity check); report the counts in the methods appendix.\n\n**Body-style encoding (contingency):**\n- If a distinct categorical column exists (e.g., `car_type` \u2208 {fastback, notchback, estateback}): one-hot encode into 3 columns appended to the vector \u2192 29 input features (per A2).\n- If body style is already baked into the 26 parameters: leave the vector as-is (no extra column), and state this in the writeup.\n- The split always stratifies on body style \u00a74 regardless of encoding branch.\n\n**Target definition at load time:** assume `Cd` is the dimensionless drag coefficient (F3). Runtime check: verify Cd values are positive and in a plausible ~0.1\u20130.5 band; if values look like a different quantity (zero/negative/out of band), halt and flag. This \"Cd-vs-CdA check\" is the explicit load-time gate the researcher requested. Prior-work RMSE figures (~0.005\u20130.010 Cd) and row counts (~8k for DrivAerNet++ vs 4k DrivAerNet) are used **only as sanity anchors for plausibility checks, never asserted as established facts**.\n\n## 4. Split policy\n\n- If the dataset ships a **canonical train/test split**, honor it exactly and note its provenance.\n- Otherwise, create our own: **stratified 80/20**, stratifying on (body style, and a Cd-quantile bin with ~5 bins) so the test set covers all three configurations and the drag range. Fixed seed (e.g., 2026).\n- The test set is **touched exactly once** for evaluation and is never used for fitting, scaling, hyperparameter search, early-stopping, or feature encoding. Its identity is fixed across all n and all models; the same rows are reused at every ladder size.\n- A small validation slice (from the training side only, ~10% of the training portion at the largest n) is used for model selection/early stopping; it is **not** the reported test metric.\n\n## 5. Training-size ladder (function of N_total)\n\nLet `N_total` = usable rows after \u00a73 cleaning, and `N_test` = test-set size under \u00a74. Practical ceiling `n_max = N_total \u2212 N_test` (at least ~80% of N_total). Ladder (log-spaced, always clamped to `min(k, n_max)`):\n\n`{100, 250, 500, 1000, 2000, 4000, 6000, n_max}` if n_max \u2265 6000;\nscaled down proportionally if the loaded N_total is smaller (e.g., {100, 250, 500, 1000, \u2026 , n_max}).\n\n- At each (model, size), the training subset of size n is drawn **without replacement** from the training portion (stratified by body style), so every curve covers the same ladder and the test set stays identical.\n- **GPR partial-curve note:** GPR with an RBF kernel is O(n\u00b3) in fitting and O(n\u00b2) memory; it is capped at `n \u2264 2000` by design (\u00a76). All other models run the full ladder. GPR is explicitly reported as a **partial-curve model**; its scaling exponent is computed on its truncated range only, and architecture comparisons involving GPR at n > 2000 are excluded (noting the ceiling asymmetry in the writeup). This is a cost-driven choice, not a fairness claim.\n\n## 6. Model set (rationale + fixed hyperparameters)\n\nAll implemented with sklearn-family regressors on the 26(+3) standardized inputs; each is fit and hyper-parameter-fixed **before** seeing n (i.e., hyperparameters are constants across the ladder, chosen once on the validation slice at a mid size, n=2000, per model \u2014 not re-tuned per n, to keep the scaling comparison clean).\n\n1. **Ridge** (linear baseline): \u03b1=1.0 (fixed). Captures whether a linear mapping's error scales with n; upper bound reference.\n2. **Polynomial degree-2 Ridge**: PolynomialFeatures(degree=2) + Ridge \u03b1=1.0 (fixed). Adds curvature; feature explosion is modest at 29 inputs (\u2248 30/40 features \u2192 ~465\u2013820 with degree-2 + one-hot; trivially cheap).\n3. **Random Forest**: 400 trees, min_samples_leaf=2, other defaults (fixed). Non-parametric, no closed-form scaling.\n4. **HistGradientBoosting** (optional): default settings, early-stopping on validation slice (constant across n). Included if compute budget allows; otherwise dropped and noted.\n5. **Gaussian Process Regression**: RBF kernel with length-scale initialized from feature std, plus WhiteKernel (noise). **Capped at n \u2264 2000** (O(n\u00b3)); partial curve per \u00a75.\n6. **Small MLP** (MLPRegressor): 2 hidden layers of 64 units each, ReLU, Adam, \u03b1=1e-4, batch-size 32, max_iter capped with early stopping on the validation slice (constant across n). Chosen to be trainable on CPU for 8k\u00d729 in seconds\u2013minutes.\n\nRationale for the set: it spans the design-space axes the question cares about \u2014 linear vs. non-linear closed-form (Ridge vs. poly-2), non-parametric ensemble (RF/HistGB), Bayesian non-parametric (GPR), and learned non-linear (MLP) \u2014 while keeping every model tabular on the 26-param input (F5).\n\n## 7. Metrics and target transform\n\n- **Primary metric:** RMSE on Cd in **natural Cd units** (evaluated after inverse-transform of the standardized target).\n- Secondary: MAE, R\u00b2, and per-body-style RMSE on the same test subsets.\n- Reported for: every (model, size, replicate); aggregated as mean \u00b1 std over replicates.\n- **Robustness check (stated, secondary):** repeat the full ladder for Ridge and MLP on `log(Cd)` target (transform back for RMSE in Cd units), to check that size-scaling conclusions are not artifacts of the target transform. Since Cd's spread is modest (plausible band \u2248 0.1\u20130.5, i.e., ~2.5\u00d7), log is a sensitivity check, not the primary choice \u2014 stated explicitly.\n\n## 8. Repeated-seed / pairing scheme\n\n- For each (model, size): 5 replicate draws of the size-n training subset, from 5 fixed seed offsets (e.g., 1000+r). Same 5 subset draws are used for **every** model at that size \u2192 models are evaluated on **identical training data**, enabling paired comparisons.\n- MLP additionally nests 5 weight-init seeds per subset draw (25 fits at each size) to isolate optimizer noise; RF bagging seed fixed per replicate.\n- Summary statistic at each (model, size) = mean \u00b1 std across the 5 subsets (25 for MLP). Paired analysis uses within-subset (within-seed) differences so model-vs-model noise is correlated and cancellable.\n\n## 9. Analysis plan\n\n**(a) Size-scaling (Claim A):**\n- Per model: fit log RMSE vs log n by OLS (only the ladder points \u2264 n_max, and for GPR only \u2264 2000); report exponent b (slope), its bootstrap 95% CI (resampling replicates), and R\u00b2 of the log-log fit.\n- Test H0: b \u2265 0 (no improvement) vs. H1: b < 0, via bootstrap; significance flag.\n- Report the full learning curve (mean \u00b1 std) for every model on one plot; report the largest n where RMSE gains (per added sample) still exceed a noise floor (bootstrap CI width).\n\n**(b) Architecture differences (Claim B):**\n- At each ladder size, paired bootstrap over the replicate/seed differences \u0394RMSE(model_i \u2212 model_j): report mean difference and 95% CI; flag non-overlap with 0.\n- Report the **rank of models by RMSE at each n** and check stability of the ranking across the ladder (does the ordering hold at n=250 as at n=n_max?). Where ranking flips, report at which n.\n- Explicit statements: \"at matched n, on the same frozen test set, X beats Y by \u0394 RMSE (95% CI [.., ..])\"; nothing asserted from point estimates alone.\n\n## 10. Controls and how each supports the claims\n\n| Control | Supports |\n|---|---|\n| Frozen identical test set (never fit/scaled/selected on) | Both A and B: any observed improvement is a genuine generalization gain; cross-model gaps are on identical rows |\n| Scaler/encoder fit on training slice only at each n | Both: no test leakage; preprocessing comparable |\n| Same subsample draws across models | B: paired, correlated-noise comparison \u2192 tighter CI on differences |\n| Same ladder of n for all models (GPR capped, flagged) | B: matched data volume excludes \"more data\" confound |\n| Hyperparameters fixed across n (tuned once at mid size) | B: differences reflect architecture, not per-n tuning luck |\n| Repeated seeds reporting mean\u00b1std | A: separates signal from subsample noise on the scaling curve |\n| Cd-vs-CdA / parameter-count load-time gates | Validity: silently wrong target or feature vector would invalidate both claims |\n| Body-style stratification in split and subsample | Validity + interpretability: all three configurations present at every n |\n\n## 11. Feasibility\n\n- Compute: 8k\u00d729 rows is negligible for Ridge/poly-2/RF/HistGB/MLP; 224 CPU cores and ~2 TB RAM are ample; no GPU needed. GPR at n=2000 (\u22488e9 flops, O(n\u00b2)\u22484M kernel) is minutes-scale per fit; 5 replicates \u00d7 ladder sizes \u22642000 is well within budget. Total MLP work (\u226425 fits \u00d7 8 sizes) is minutes.\n- Environment requirements for a future run: mount DrivAerNet++ attribute tables; install numpy/scipy/scikit-learn (pip offline impossible here \u2014 must be preprovisioned). Documented, not a blocker for design.\n\n## 12. Assumptions, uncertainties, and contingency summary\n\nAssumptions (not locally verifiable; stated as assumptions, not fact):\n- A1. Dataset ships tabular rows with the 26 params and Cd (C-Schema-1/2). If only meshes/CFD sources exist, the experiment cannot run without rerunning CFD (out of scope) \u2014 flag, do not fabricate.\n- A2. Body style is a separate categorical column (one-hot branch); fallback: if baked into parameters, use vector as-is (\u00a73).\n- A3. Ground-truth Cd exists per row from CFD within the dataset (per the question's framing); enforced by the load-time plausibility gate.\n- A4. Row count is in the low thousands (8k nominal anchor) \u2014 the ladder adapts via n_max = N_total \u2212 N_test regardless.\n- A5. No canonical split assumed; we honor one only if it ships (\u00a74).\n- A6. Parameter semantics/ranges are whatever the mounted metadata defines; the design never hardcodes bounds.\n\nUncertainties flagged, each with a contingency branch in this document: parameter count \u226026 (halt: C-Schema-4); missing Cd (halt: C-Schema-3); body-style encoding (two branches, \u00a73); sample count (ladder function, \u00a75); canonical split (honor-or-create, \u00a74); offline bare environment (documented resource requirement, \u00a711).\n\n## 13. Deliverables of the experiment (when run)\n\n1. Learning-curve figure: test RMSE (mean\u00b1std) vs n, per model, log-log, with GPR partial range marked.\n2. Table of scaling exponents b with bootstrap CIs and significance flags.\n3. Table of paired bootstrap \u0394RMSE between every model pair at every ladder size, with CIs.\n4. Ranking-stability summary across n.\n5. Per-body-style RMSE breakdown (fastback/notchback/estateback) at the ceiling size.\n6. Robustness appendix: log-Cd sensitivity run (Ridge, MLP) and any data-cleaning/halted-branch reports.\n7. Written verdict on Claim A (size-scaling: yes/no, with exponent) and Claim B (architecture differences: yes/no, which models, at which n), with the control table above as evidence mapping.", "evidence": "Sandbox inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; filesystem-wide searc